WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.860
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about the
pandemic.

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:07.350
We are embroiled right now in a battle between
coronavirus vaccines and something known as

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:08.879
the Delta covid variant.

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:13.150
We're also going to talk more about the Delta
variant on today's bonus show.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.790
We have an extraordinarily strong situation
here in the United States against the virus,

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.900
as I will show you shortly.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.880
But there are real warning signs globally
right now which signal that unless we keep

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.760
vaccinating and really vaccinate more people
more quickly, we could be in a real problematic

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:33.680
situation very soon, within a couple of months.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.820
So let me walk you through it.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:40.489
At first glance, if you look at us covid cases,
you see that cases have dropped now more than

00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:45.460
95 percent from the pandemic high of about
two hundred and fifty five thousand cases

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.490
a day now down to about 11000 cases a day.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:53.850
That is a massive reduction, more than 95
percent when you look at covid deaths in the

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.180
United States.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.700
You also see really strong numbers.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:04.129
In fact, deaths are down more than 91 percent
from their pandemic, high of more than 30,

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.640
400 per day to just 300 deaths per day.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.070
Now, these are fantastic numbers.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.800
We are getting down to what we would call
like baseline flu level risk.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.860
Now, when you look globally, it still looks
good, but it's not quite as good.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:25.640
And what I mean by that is that while globally
cases are still down, they're decreasing more

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.470
and more slowly.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.890
Global cases peaked around 830000.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:38.260
We're now down about 56 percent to about four
hundred and seventy thousand global covid

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.740
cases per day.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:43.820
But if you're looking at the charts that we're
displaying here, you'll see the decline is

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:44.820
leveling out.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.520
It's a decline, but it's a slower and slower
decline.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.850
And similarly on global deaths, we see deaths
are now down globally, about 44 percent from

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:53.850
their peak.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:59.410
But just in the last few days, that decline
has also started to slow down a little bit.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:00.410
So what's happening here?

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:01.780
What's driving this?

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.400
What's happening is that a more contagious
by about 60 percent variant called the Delta

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:11.230
variant is spreading quickly in some countries.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:16.610
This variant, as we talked about last week
with Dr. Ashish Jha, this is a variant that

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:20.150
not only spread 60 percent more quickly, it's
becoming dominant.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:24.730
If we look at cases in the United Kingdom,
cases are going up.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:30.680
Now, this is not cases are going down, but
more slowly, we are actually seeing an increase

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:32.379
in cases in the United Kingdom.

00:02:32.379 --> 00:02:35.700
They were down to under 2000 new cases a day.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.290
They're now back up to almost 10000 cases
a day.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.840
Now, you might see this in, say, David, this
is no problem.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.890
You know why?

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:45.819
Because in the U.S., we've vaccinated way
more people than in the United Kingdom.

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:46.950
So we don't have to worry.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.270
And you would be 100 percent wrong.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:56.220
If you look at vaccination rates by country,
you see that the US has fully vaccinated 45

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:03.860
percent of our population and the UK has fully
vaccinated more, 47 percent of its population.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:09.030
It is wrong to say we don't have to worry
about Delta because we vaccinated more people.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.160
We haven't.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:16.430
What's happening is that the Delta variant
is dominant in the UK already and in the US

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:18.060
it is not yet dominant.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.269
This variant started in India.

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:24.060
60 percent of new cases in the UK are now
the Delta variant.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:27.770
Only 10 percent of new cases in the US are
now the Delta variant.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:34.510
So what that means is we are simply on a delay
from what is happening in the United Kingdom.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.190
Now, let's be really, really clear.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:42.330
If you were vaccinated, at least with the
vaccines approved here, Fizer, Moderna or

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.800
Johnson and Johnson, you were protected against
the Delta variant.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.260
The vaccines work really well against the
Delta variant.

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:54.150
The problem is that 55 percent of the country
has no protection right now.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:04:00.730
And so when you say, well, our pool of vaccinated
people is half the country and this variant

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:06.090
spread 60 percent more easily, you are going
to get more and more cases among the unvaccinated,

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:11.750
but you will also get more cases among the
vaccinated because vaccination is not 100

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:12.750
percent.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:13.750
It's just simple math.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:19.510
If you have a vaccine that's 95 percent effective,
when there aren't many cases around you, that

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.979
five percent that's not protected is less
likely to get the virus.

00:04:23.979 --> 00:04:27.949
If you have a 95 percent effective vaccine
and there's a ton of cases around you because

00:04:27.949 --> 00:04:33.749
of unvaccinated people, it's just statistically
more likely you will have an interaction with

00:04:33.749 --> 00:04:36.559
an unvaccinated and possibly infected person.

00:04:36.559 --> 00:04:39.719
And you'll see more of what are called breakthrough
cases.

00:04:39.719 --> 00:04:47.059
Never mind, of course, that among the unvaccinated,
if something is 60 percent more contagious,

00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:50.460
it's going to spread like wildfire among the
unvaccinated.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.369
So this is now a race.

00:04:52.369 --> 00:04:58.310
If we can keep vaccinating, maybe we can suppress
the Delta variant better than the UK simply

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.759
because we're behind where they are in terms
of the spread of that variant becoming dominant.

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:07.270
It's, you could say, a geographical thing.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:11.529
It got to the UK first, 60 percent of their
new cases are now Delta.

00:05:11.529 --> 00:05:13.499
Only 10 percent of ours are.

00:05:13.499 --> 00:05:14.930
It's still here.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:16.210
It's still spreading.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.120
The percentage of cases here that are Delta
is growing.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:25.649
But we have the benefit of you know, it seems
like it's probably a couple of months of time

00:05:25.649 --> 00:05:27.499
to get more and more people vaccinated.

00:05:27.499 --> 00:05:29.349
That's the fundamental battle right now.

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:30.979
I'd get vaccinated.

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:31.990
I did.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.130
And if I were you, I would do the exact same
thing.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:38.589
Let's now talk a little bit about the evolution
of a vaccine opposition globally

